# Analysis of HKSAR v. Chan Ming Lok [2009] HKCFI 571; HCMA 225/2009

## 1. Summary of Facts

The appellant, Chan Ming Lok, was charged with possession of an offensive weapon contrary to section 33(1) of the Public Order Ordinance (Cap. 245). The charge alleged that on 29 June 2008, in a public place outside Nos. 9-11 Cochrane Street, Central, Hong Kong, without lawful authority or reasonable excuse, he had with him an offensive weapon, namely a knife.

At approximately 1:45 a.m., Police Constable PW2 observed the appellant walking in the vicinity of the rear alley of No. 2 Kau Yu Fong towards Aberdeen Street. Given the late hour, the officer found the appellant suspicious and radioed his colleague (PW1). PW2 followed the appellant as he walked along Aberdeen Street, stopped at the junction with Wellington Street, looked left and right, then turned right onto Wellington Street. The appellant stopped again at Peel Street, removed his rucksack from his left shoulder to his right shoulder, and rested his right hand on the lower part of the rucksack. He continued walking, stopped at Gutzlaff Street, looked around, and turned into Gutzlaff Street where he walked slowly for about 15-20 metres before turning back to Wellington Street and heading towards Cochrane Street. PW1 then intercepted the appellant outside Nos. 9-11 Cochrane Street.

When questioned about the knife in his bag, the appellant initially did not answer. He later stated he had gone there to buy food and explained that the knife had previously been used by him as a cook. He said he lived in Shenzhen and usually returned late at night alone, and because public order was bad, he was afraid of being robbed and carried the knife for self-defence. He claimed he usually went through Customs at the border carrying the rucksack and did not know he was not allowed to bring the knife. When cautioned, he replied that he had brought the knife through Customs many times, only used it to protect himself and for self-defence, not for any other purpose.

The magistrate found the knife was an offensive weapon and that the appellant had no lawful authority or reasonable excuse for possessing it. The appellant was convicted and sentenced to 6 months' imprisonment.

## 2. Summary of Arguments

**Appellant's Arguments (Mr Wong):**

Mr Wong argued that the magistrate wrongly concluded the knife was an offensive weapon on the basis that it was an article "suitable for causing injury to the person." He relied on the obiter suggestion of Bokhary JA (as he then was) in *R v. Chong Ah Choi & Others* [1994] 2 HKCLR 263, that to comply with the Hong Kong Bill of Rights Ordinance (Cap. 383), the definition of offensive weapon in section 2 of the Public Order Ordinance might have to be construed as if the reference to weapons "suitable... for causing injury to the person" had been repealed.

Mr Wong further argued that paragraph 20 of the magistrate's Statement of Findings, which concluded that the knife was an offensive weapon, contained the basis for that conclusion. The magistrate expressly found the knife did not fall within categories (a) and (b). Mr Wong suggested that the magistrate's reference to the knife being "pointed and quite sharp and could cause injury to the person," combined with the absence of any finding as to the appellant's intention, must lead to the inference that the magistrate found it was an offensive weapon by reason of being "suitable for causing injury to the person"—a category that should no longer exist. On Mr Wong's submission, paragraph 21 concerned only whether the appellant had lawful authority or reasonable excuse for possessing what the magistrate had already found to be an offensive weapon.

**Respondent's Arguments (Miss Lam):**

Miss Lam for the respondent stated at the outset that the Secretary of Justice accepted that there are now only three categories of offensive weapons—(a), (b), and (d)—and would not argue that category (c) still survived.

Miss Lam conceded that the magistrate's findings were not ideally set out but argued that the magistrate was very experienced and would have been familiar with the nature of the offence. She argued that since the magistrate did not refer to a fourth category of offensive weapon in paragraph 20, she must have proceeded on the basis that there were only three categories. Therefore, her reference to the fact that the knife could cause injury to the person must have been the basis for a finding that the appellant did intend it for such use, although she had not said so expressly. Miss Lam also referred to the penultimate sentence in paragraph 21 as support for that view.

## 3. Legal Principles (Ratio Decidendi)

The case engages several significant legal principles concerning the definition and prosecution of offensive weapon offences under the Public Order Ordinance, the effect of the Bill of Rights Ordinance on statutory definitions, and the approach of an appellate court to ambiguous magisterial findings.

**The Definition of "Offensive Weapon" and the Effect of the Bill of Rights Ordinance**

Section 2 of the Public Order Ordinance defines "offensive weapons" as "any article made, or adapted for use, or suitable for causing injury to the person, or intended by the person having it in his possession or under his control for such use by him or another person." As drafted, the Ordinance provides four categories of offensive weapons: (a) an article made for causing injury to the person; (b) an article adapted for use for causing injury to the person; (c) an article suitable for causing injury to the person; and (d) an article intended by the person having it with him for use for causing injury to the person.

The court accepted, based on the respondent's concession and the earlier obiter dicta in *Chong Ah Choi*, that category (c)—articles "suitable for causing injury to the person"—has been impliedly repealed by the operation of the Bill of Rights Ordinance. This means that the mere fact that an article is capable of causing injury (i.e., that it is "suitable for causing injury") is insufficient to establish that it is an offensive weapon. The prosecution must instead prove that the article falls within one of the remaining three categories: that it was made for causing injury, adapted for such use, or intended by the person having it for such use. This is a significant and controversial point because it means that the statutory definition as enacted by the legislature has been partially invalidated by the court's interpretation to bring it into conformity with the Bill of Rights, rather than the legislature having amended the provision itself.

**The Trap of Unamended Legislation**

The court made a striking observation that despite the comments of the Court of Appeal in *Chong Ah Choi* being made over 14 years earlier, section 2 of the Public Order Ordinance had not yet been amended. The court described the unamended provision as "a trap to the unwary." This principle underscores that where a statutory provision has been authoritatively construed as partially invalid or inapplicable due to constitutional or Bill of Rights considerations, the failure of the legislature to amend the provision creates ongoing uncertainty and potential injustice for defendants and legal practitioners alike.

**The Importance of Clear Factual Findings by the Trial Magistrate**

A central legal principle arising from this case is the requirement for magistrates to make clear and unambiguous findings as to the basis upon which they conclude an article is an offensive weapon. The magistrate in this case found that the knife was an offensive weapon without stating into which category she was satisfied it fell. She expressly found that the knife did not fall within categories (a) and (b), and referred to the fact that it was "pointed and quite sharp and could cause injury to the person." The appellate court found that this reasoning was consistent with a finding under the now-repealed category (c)—that the knife was "suitable for causing injury"—rather than a finding under category (d)—that the appellant intended it for use for causing injury.

The court held that where the magistrate's findings are ambiguous as to the legal basis for a conviction, and where it cannot be excluded that the magistrate proceeded on an incorrect legal basis (i.e., the repealed category (c)), the conviction cannot stand on that basis alone. This principle emphasizes the importance of precision in magisterial findings and the appellate court's duty to scrutinize the legal foundation of a conviction, even where the evidence might otherwise support a conviction on a different basis.

**The Appellate Court's Power to Review and Substitute Findings**

Despite finding that the magistrate may have proceeded on the wrong basis, the court held that this being an appeal by way of rehearing on the papers, it must consider whether, bearing in mind the magistrate's findings as to the credibility of witnesses, the conviction should nonetheless stand. The court reviewed the evidence and found that the appellant had admitted he carried the knife for self-defence, which necessarily implied an intention that it be used as a weapon against another person. The court held that even if the knife was only to be used in self-defence as the appellant claimed, there was always the possibility that the knife would cause injuries to someone. By choosing to have such a knife with him in circumstances that he knew it might cause injuries to someone, the court was satisfied that he had it in his possession intending to cause injury to a person. The fact that he anticipated such use might occur in the Mainland rather than Hong Kong was irrelevant.

This principle confirms that an intention to use an article for self-defence (even in another jurisdiction) can satisfy the mens rea requirement for category (d) of the definition, and that the location where the intended use might occur is immaterial to the offence of possession in Hong Kong.

**Self-Defence and "Reasonable Excuse"**

The court confirmed that self-defence does not amount to a reasonable excuse for possession of an offensive weapon where there is no evidence of imminent danger. The appellant's claim that he carried the knife for self-defence in China, where public order was bad, was rejected as providing no lawful authority or reasonable excuse. This principle reaffirms that the defence of "reasonable excuse" under section 33(1) requires an objective assessment of the circumstances, and a generalized fear of robbery without evidence of imminent danger is insufficient.

**Sentencing Principles for Offensive Weapon Offences**

On sentence, the court applied the principle that while the carrying of offensive weapons must be viewed seriously—as one of the most important factors in rendering Hong Kong's streets safe is the absence of such weapons—a sentence must be tailored to the circumstances of the individual case. The court considered the following factors relevant to reducing the sentence from 6 to 4 months: the defendant had never before been sentenced to imprisonment; the knife was a relatively small domestic knife; the court could not exclude the possibility that the appellant intended it as a weapon of defence rather than offence; there was no evidence of any connection between his movements on the night in question and the knife, as he worked as a chef in the close vicinity and had finished work not long before; there was no evidence he was following anyone; and he had no similar convictions. The court emphasized that these circumstances provided no excuse for carrying the knife but were relevant to sentence, and reiterated that the law views the carrying of such weapons very seriously even if carried in self-defence.

## 4. Striking, Unusual, or Remarkable Aspects

Several aspects of this case are striking or remarkable:

**The Legislative Inertia:** Perhaps the most remarkable feature is the court's explicit observation that despite the Court of Appeal's comments in *Chong Ah Choi* being made over 14 years earlier, section 2 of the Public Order Ordinance had still not been amended. The court described the unamended provision as "a trap to the unwary." This highlights a significant and unusual gap between judicial interpretation and legislative action, leaving a statutory provision on the books that is partially invalid but unreformed, creating ongoing legal uncertainty for defendants, prosecutors, and magistrates alike.

**The Respondent's Concession:** It is noteworthy that the Secretary of Justice, through Miss Lam, conceded at the outset that only three categories of offensive weapons now survive and expressly declined to argue that category (c) still existed. This concession reflects the prosecutorial acceptance of the *Chong Ah Choi* position, even though the legislature has not acted. It is unusual for the government to concede the partial invalidity of its own ordinance without legislative amendment.

**The Magistrate's Ambiguity:** The case is remarkable for the appellate court's finding that the magistrate—described as "otherwise" setting out her findings "with exemplary clarity"—failed to specify the legal category under which she found the knife to be an offensive weapon. This ambiguity, combined with the magistrate's express finding that the knife did not fall within categories (a) and (b), created a situation where the appellate court could not exclude the possibility that the conviction was based on a repealed legal category. This underscores the practical difficulties created by the unamended legislation and the importance of precise fact-finding at trial.

**The "Self-Defence in China" Argument:** The appellant's argument that he carried the knife for self-defence in China, and therefore had no intention to use it to injure anyone in Hong Kong, is unusual. The court's rejection of this argument—holding that the location of intended use is irrelevant—is a notable principle. It confirms that the offence of possession in Hong Kong is complete regardless of where the defendant intended to use the weapon, and that an intention to use a knife for self-defence (even abroad) can satisfy the mens rea for the offence.

**The Sentencing Reduction:** The reduction of sentence from 6 to 4 months is remarkable for the court's nuanced approach. While the court acknowledged the seriousness of carrying offensive weapons and the importance of keeping Hong Kong's streets safe, it also considered the appellant's personal circumstances—that he was a chef who had finished work nearby, that there was no evidence connecting his suspicious movements to the knife, and that he had no prior similar convictions. This balancing exercise is a useful illustration of sentencing principles in offensive weapon cases, particularly the relevance of the defendant's occupation and the absence of evidence of intended violence.

**The "Trap to the Unwary" Language:** The court's use of the phrase "a trap to the unwary" is striking and memorable. It vividly captures the practical injustice created by the legislature's failure to amend the Ordinance, and serves as a judicial warning to all those who might rely on the unamended statutory text. This language is unusual in its candour and directness, and it underscores the court's frustration with the legislative inaction.

In conclusion, *HKSAR v. Chan Ming Lok* is a significant case that illustrates the ongoing consequences of the *Chong Ah Choi* interpretation of section 2 of the Public Order Ordinance, the importance of precise magisterial findings, the irrelevance of the location of intended use to the offence of possession, and the nuanced approach to sentencing in offensive weapon cases. Its most remarkable feature is the court's explicit criticism of the legislature's 14-year failure to amend a provision that has been judicially construed as partially invalid, leaving a "trap to the unwary" on the statute book. [end of text]


